#
# prima-materia — the Bashar transmission, entered at Blueprint for Change
#
# Opened from the structure-driven entrance of SPEC §14: pmt:BasharTransmission
# was registered at pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-channeled.ttl with no
# node, and its registration names the corpus as operative, steps given with
# stated conditions of failure; that is the testability-driven entrance as
# well. What was opened is Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, read complete in
# a full-text layer that carries neither title page, imprint nor page numbers.
# The recorded session transcripts are unopened and the tradition says so.
#
# Order of the file: the work; the tradition with the three decisions; then
# the harvest in the order of the work, chapter by chapter.
#
# Citation. By chapter and section heading; openings of chapters before the
# first heading are cited as such. No page is given anywhere in this file.
#
# Who speaks. A claim made by Bashar is the work's claim. Questioners' own
# reports of a result are entered as third-party attestation. Chapter 12 is
# given to an entity of Orion and the end of chapter 15 to the Facilitators of
# Zeta Reticuli, each with its own pm:Receiving.
#
# Identifiers. Four readers took one stretch each (chapters 1-5, 6-8, 9-12,
# 13-15). Where the same being or term is entered from two stretches, the later
# node carries the chapter (pmc:BasharShakanaNamingChapter11): one name, two
# places. Signs of success and conditions of failure carry a note beginning
# "Counted as a sign of success:" or "Counted as a condition of failure:".
#

#
# The work
#

pmw:BasharBlueprintForChange a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Bashar: Blueprint for Change. A Message from Our Future"@en, "Bashar: Blueprint for Change. Eine Botschaft aus unserer Zukunft"@de ;
    skos:note "Darryl Anka. The full-text layer read (archive.org darryl-anka-bashar-blueprint-for-change-a-message-from-our-future-new-solutions-pub-1990_202406, 579312 characters, ASCII letter share 1.0) begins at Chapter One and carries no title page, imprint, copyright line or page numbers, so the edition is not established from the text; the identifier of the upload names a publication of 1990, which this file does not use as a date. Two further identifiers of the same file (without the suffix, and bashar-blueprint-for-change-a-message-from-our-future) have the same size and were not read separately. Fifteen chapters; dated sessions are chapter 3 (August 1987) and chapter 10 (Whole Life Expo, November 1989), the others undated in the layer; the latest event named is a visit in the past month to Billy Meier in chapter 14."@en ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-10-27 by four readers, one stretch each, at the wording; one pass per stretch and no replacement guarantee after SPEC §15 'Ernte im Maßstab', so the harvest claims completeness only as far as the reading notes in its pull request go. The layer is clean apart from '|' for 'I' throughout, scattered misread letters, scene breaks of garbled asterisks, a coloured diagram in chapter 3 (the harmonic wave) that is absent, and section headings that run into the next question; no stretch of running text is missing. Not entered: session chit-chat and jokes, cosmological narrative without a practice or capacity (the civilization that could not forget, Star Wars as a past cycle, Maldek, the physical figures, schooling, music and craft interiors of Essassani), the questioners' personal charts."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvest counts: twenty-nine signs of success and thirty-six conditions of failure, counted apart. Ten of the failures carry a date and all ten are predictions about the world with public outcomes, among them open contact within thirty to fifty years from about 1987, a radiation-binding polymer within twelve to fourteen years, a world government founded by 2011 to 2013, every person on the planet joining the noon minute of the Peace Clock by 1 January 2000, a threshold after 15 October 1989 and a deadline in 1997; four of the signs carry a time (the Peace Clock's year 2000, things falling into place immediately, fear of the visitors becoming legend in a very short time, help at the dream level noticed within three years). None of the conditions of an operation sets a count. Of the undated failures all but one put the failure on the person, on belief, expectation, fear, waiting or wording; the one exception is the visitors' timing. Several passages name the obvious failure in advance and declare that it is none: acting and nothing happening, understanding without feeling different, the old inner voices returning, no memory of an astral visit, not seeing a craft. And chapter 4 states that every prediction is a reading of present probability that the energy can change (pmc:BasharPredictionsAsProbability), which makes each dated failure readable as a change of energy rather than a miss."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:BasharTransmission a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BasharBlueprintForChange ;
    rdfs:label "Bashar transmission"@en, "Bashar-Überlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission received by Darryl Anka since 1984, teaching that acting on the strongest available excitement is the means by which one comes to occupy a different reality."@en ;
    skos:note "Unusually operative for a channelled corpus: the teaching is given as steps with stated conditions of failure, in front of audiences, on record."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, the first of the three decisions SPEC §14 requires, kept at documented, and the route is printed in the work. The sessions answer questions framed by the contact and abduction literature of their years, which questioners name: Whitley Strieber's Communion (ch. 13), Budd Hopkins' Intruders (ch. 15), Billy Meier's Pleiadian contacts (ch. 14), and the Zeta Reticuli, Pleiadians, Sirius and Orion as the cast of that literature; chapter 9 cites Barbara Sher's Wishcraft (1979) in a footnote for its method. Agreement with the contactee transmissions, the Ra material or the Seth material is therefore reception unless shown otherwise."@en ;
    skos:note "Recension and edition, the second decision: Blueprint for Change in the one full-text layer reachable, which has no imprint; cited by chapter and section heading. The session recordings and the transcripts sold by the channel's organisation were not compared, and a printed copy with title page would settle the edition."@en ;
    skos:note "The third decision, the number of citable places: Blueprint for Change read complete, 553 nodes in the order of the work, 132 from chapters 1-5, 139 from chapters 6-8, 123 from chapters 9-12 and 159 from chapters 13-15, each at a chapter and section."@en ;
    skos:note "Open: the recorded session transcripts named in the registration are not opened and are not a work with an identity the graph could carry; a later run that opens one declares it as a work here."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change; the recorded session transcripts" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:BasharBlueprintForChange ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 1 Masters of Limitation, opening; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers" .

#
# Chapter 1, Masters of Limitation
#

pmc:BasharBlueprintReceiving a pm:Receiving ;
    rdfs:label "The work's claim that its answers are Bashar's, spoken through Darryl Anka"@en, "Die Behauptung des Werkes, seine Antworten seien Bashars, gesprochen durch Darryl Anka"@de ;
    skos:definition "The book presents itself as questions from session audiences answered by Bashar, a being of Essassani, through a human channel. Bashar says that though one identity seems to speak, very many consciousnesses of the Association of Worlds join his awareness telempathically for each session, so that a gathering sits on his side too. His people speak through this format to stay one step removed, so that the message counts more than the messenger. Without the model of the physical channel, through whom they experience Earth life in what humans would call a past life of Bashar's, their thought would be too alien to help or to speak in human terms."@en ;
    skos:note "This node records the claim, not its truth. In chapters 1 to 5 of the full-text layer the receiver is never named; he is called the physical channel or the channel, and the attribution to Darryl Anka rests on the work's authorship. The speaker declines to prove who he is, saying the sessions exist to validate the listeners' existence, not his, and that he cannot force belief."@en ;
    skos:note "Bashar's own account of where his statements come from, given when asked how he knows what Jesus said: his people sense into humanity's mass consciousness and higher consciousness, and anyone can consult his own higher consciousness and hear the same. Elsewhere in these chapters he speaks of his society as telepathic and of telepathy as telempathy activated by love."@en ;
    skos:note "Later chapters, entered by other readers of this run, add to the circumstances: in chapter 8 a questioner speaks of the sessions as held at eight every Thursday night and Bashar of the ideas shared over the past two years; chapter 10 is a session at the Whole Life Expo of November 1989; in chapter 11 a questioner names Darryl as the voice through which Bashar's counterpart Anima has been heard, and Bashar calls the channel his own one past life on Earth (pmc:BasharFutureSelfOfTheChannel); chapter 12 gives the channel to an entity of Orion and the end of chapter 15 to the Facilitators of Zeta Reticuli, both with Bashar's facilitation (pmc:BasharNeamonReceiving, pmc:BasharFacilitatorsReceiving)."@en ;
    pm:receivedBy "Darryl Anka, referred to in chapters 1 to 5 only as the physical channel or the channel and named by questioners from chapter 11" ;
    pm:claimedSpeaker "Bashar, of the civilization of Essassani, speaking as ambassador for the Association of Worlds, whose many members are said to be linked into his consciousness during each session" ;
    pm:receptionCircumstance "Public question-and-answer sessions before audiences; the questions are the audience's and are set off in the print, plain in the full-text layer, with interjections from other listeners marked (Other). Chapter 3 is dated August 1987 and is in part a lecture keyed to a diagram not reproduced in the layer; one question in chapter 4 is dated 1 November, and bracketed insertions such as (1987), (California) and (Star Trek.) are an editor's or the audience's. The speaker calls the channel's Earth life a past life of his own and the source of the terms he uses. Chapter 3 reports a dream communication in which Bashar's people took part through the channel, who remembered the dream symbolically. Who transcribed and edited the sessions is not stated in these chapters, nor whether recordings survive." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BasharTransmission ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BasharBlueprintForChange ;
    dcterms:source "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 1 Masters of Limitation, opening section; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers", "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 1 Masters of Limitation, section How Could You Forget?; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers", "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 3 Harmonic Wave (August, 1987), section The Dream; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers", "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 5 The Second Coming, section The Second Coming; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers", "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 5 The Second Coming, section The Only Son; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers" .

pmc:BasharNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Bashar, speaker for the Association of Worlds"@en, "Bashar, Sprecher der Vereinigung der Welten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The speaker of the book answers to the name Bashar. Though heard as one identity, he says he stands for a very large number of consciousnesses of the Association of Worlds who join his awareness, telempathically as he puts it, for each session, so that a gathering sits on his side as on the audience's. He belongs to the world he calls Essassani, calls himself an ambassador for all who listen on that side, and says the human he speaks through was, in a sense, one of his own past lives."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Bashar" ;
    pm:nameRole "a being of the civilization of Essassani, ambassador and spokesman of the Association of Worlds, speaking through Darryl Anka, whom he calls a past life of his own" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BasharTransmission ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BasharBlueprintForChange ;
    dcterms:source "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 1 Masters of Limitation, opening section; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers", "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 1 Masters of Limitation, section How Could You Forget?; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers", "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 1 Masters of Limitation, closing passage; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers" .

pmc:BasharAssociationOfWorldsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "The Association of Worlds"@en, "Die Vereinigung der Welten"@de ;
    skos:definition "A body of civilizations whose members, numbering in the hundreds of thousands or millions of consciousnesses, are said to connect into Bashar's awareness during the sessions. Elsewhere in the chapter it is called simply The Association, which other civilizations come into contact with and exchange information through."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Association of Worlds; The Association" ;
    pm:nameRole "an association of extraterrestrial civilizations, for which Bashar speaks" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BasharTransmission ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BasharBlueprintForChange ;
    dcterms:source "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 1 Masters of Limitation, opening section; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers", "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change, ch. 1 Masters of Limitation, section How Could You Forget?; read in a full-text layer without title page or page numbers" .

